# .cursorrules — .NET Framework 4.8 Master Architect

## Context
This is a .NET Framework 4.8/4.8.1 codebase. Maximum C# 7.3. No records, no Span<T>, no Minimal APIs.

## Architecture
- Domain Layer: Pure POCOs, no System.Web or System.Windows.Forms dependencies
- Application Layer: Use cases / services
- Infrastructure: EF6, ADO.NET, WCF clients
- Presentation: MVC Controllers, WebForms Code-Behind, WPF Views, WinForms Forms
- ACL: Anti-Corruption Layer for modern API integration

## Patterns
- Use Result<T> for business logic failures, not exceptions
- Always use ConfigureAwait(false) in library code
- Never use .Result or .Wait() on UI or ASP.NET threads
- Implement MVP for WinForms, MVVM for WPF
- Use Autofac/Ninject/Unity for DI
- Constructor injection with null guards (?? throw)
- Guard clauses with early returns
- Immutable value objects: sealed class + readonly fields + IEquatable<T>
- IReadOnlyList<T> for collection exposure

## Testing (Mandatory)
- TDD for new code: Red-Green-Refactor
- Characterization tests BEFORE touching legacy code
- Unit tests: NUnit/xUnit + Moq
- Integration tests: LocalDB + transaction rollback
- Coverage: New code >80%, legacy >60%, critical paths 100%
- Test presenters/ViewModels, NEVER UI controls directly

## Security
- TLS 1.2 only. Do not suggest TLS 1.3.
- Parameterized queries only. No string concatenation for SQL.
- Encrypt web.config sections with DPAPI
- ValidateAntiForgeryToken on all POST actions
- JWT via System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt (compatible version)

## Anti-patterns to Avoid
- async void (except event handlers)
- .Result or .Wait() on UI/ASP.NET threads
- ServiceLocator pattern
- Mutable static state
- God classes / spaghetti code
- Code-behind with direct SQL calls
- PolySharp or unofficial backports in production

## Migration
- Extract business logic to .NET Standard 2.0 libraries first
- Use Strangler Fig pattern for gradual migration
- EF6 + EF Core can coexist during transition
- Use YARP for routing between legacy and modern
